// automations.memql -- the referencepack reference pack.
//
// One automation that HOOKS A CORE SERVICE EVENT: it triggers on the
// creation of a core `v1:notes:note` row -- a node owned by a core memQL
// domain, not by this pack. This is the "automation hooking a core service"
// half of the pack acceptance: a pack reacts to a core concept's lifecycle
// event without the core knowing the pack exists.
//
// (Originally hooked `v1:cognition:space`, but the space concept + its
// lifecycle mutations moved out of engine core into the CoPresent pack in
// #2038. `v1:notes:note` is a still-core concept whose `createNote` mutation
// stamps `ownerUserId: actor.userId`, so it demonstrates the exact same
// pack-hooks-a-core-event seam against a concept the engine still owns. #2342)
//
// The step calls the pack's own builtin (referencePackComposeGreeting),
// so the automation also exercises the pack's Go capability. We greet the
// new note's owner. The event payload binds to the automation's typed
// `args { }` contract (G5, memql#2367): `ownerUserId` is the owner stamped
// by the core create mutation, read as a bare field.
//
// NOTE: this is a demonstration of the wiring, not a production behavior
// -- the reference pack is never linked into a production binary (it has
// no unconditional self-registering init(); see ../pack.go and
// ../register_referencepack.go).

use referencepack.builtins.{ referencePackComposeGreeting }

@enabled
@trigger(event="node.created", concept="v1:notes:note", partition="*")
@description("On creation of a core note, compose a greeting for the note owner via the pack's integration capability. Demonstrates a pack automation hooking a core service event.")
automation referencePackGreetOnNoteCreate {
  args {
    ownerUserId any @description("The note owner the greeting targets, bound from the created note's payload (G5 typed contract).")
  }

  // FIRST-CLASS PRECONDITION (Epic 4 / memql#2139). A deterministic check
  // that must hold before the steps run. On a miss the harness aborts the
  // run cleanly and emits healing.precondition.missed -- the clean self-
  // healing repair trigger AND the cross-machine portability signal. Here
  // the asserted literal is the owner id stamped by the core create
  // mutation: a note created without an ownerUserId (a portability /
  // wiring drift on some other machine) MISSES this precondition, and the
  // repair loop is handed a structured signal naming the construct, the
  // failed check, and the asserted literal -- rather than the step
  // silently greeting a blank user.
  precondition ownerPresent {
    check: exists(args.ownerUserId)
    literal: ownerUserId
    description: "The created note must carry the owner id the greeting targets."
  }
  step greet {
    builtin referencePackComposeGreeting ( userName: ownerUserId )
  }
}
